been registered in advance — only that something can produce a policy when asked.

## Hint 2

The delegating shape:

    public class TenantPolicyProvider(IOptions<AuthorizationOptions> options)
        : IAuthorizationPolicyProvider
    {
        private readonly DefaultAuthorizationPolicyProvider _fallback = new(options);

        public Task<AuthorizationPolicy> GetDefaultPolicyAsync() =>
            _fallback.GetDefaultPolicyAsync();

        public Task<AuthorizationPolicy?> GetFallbackPolicyAsync() =>
            _fallback.GetFallbackPolicyAsync();

        public Task<AuthorizationPolicy?> GetPolicyAsync(string name) =>
            name.StartsWith("tenant:") ? Build(name) : _fallback.GetPolicyAsync(name);
    }

## Hint 3

`policyName["permission:".Length..]` gives the permission. The decision left to
you: should an empty permission — the bare name `"permission:"` — build a policy
requiring a `perm` claim with an empty value, or delegate, or throw? All three
are defensible and only one of them fails loudly.

