## Hint 1

There is exactly one place where "the policy failed" becomes "this HTTP
response", and it is a service you can replace. Everything about the default
403's emptiness is a decision made in that one class.

## Hint 2

Wrapping rather than replacing:

    public class MyHandler : IAuthorizationMiddlewareResultHandler
    {
        private readonly AuthorizationMiddlewareResultHandler _inner = new();

        public async Task HandleAsync(
            RequestDelegate next, HttpContext http,
            AuthorizationPolicy policy, PolicyAuthorizationResult result)
        {
            if (!result.Forbidden) { await _inner.HandleAsync(next, http, policy, result); return; }
            // … write your own 403 …
        }
    }

Note the `next` parameter: calling the inner handler on success is what lets the
request continue to the endpoint.

## Hint 3

`http.GetEndpoint()?.Metadata.GetOrderedMetadata<IAuthorizeData>()` gives you the
authorization metadata, and `.Select(d => d.Policy).FirstOrDefault(p => p is not
null)` gives the name. The decision left to you: what should `policy` say for an
endpoint protected by the fallback policy, where there is no metadata at all?

